PyTorch (ROCm/pytorch release/2.8, commit d618db8): compile torch's HIP
code with rocPRIM in a private namespace.

WHY
On gfx803, every torch op built on rocPRIM's lookback scan, radix or merge
sort, transform or reduce-by-key kernels fails with "HIP error: invalid
device function": nonzero, boolean-mask indexing (x[mask], masked_select),
unique, and sort/argsort of more than 4096 elements, at any input size.
To reproduce: (torch.randn(10, device="cuda") > 0).nonzero().

rocPRIM is header-only, so each library compiled with it carries its own
copies of the rocPRIM kernels it uses and exports their host-side handles
with default visibility. ROCm's rocSPARSE, which libtorch_hip links,
exports 31 of the same kernel handles that libtorch_hip does (nm -D on
both). The dynamic linker binds both libraries' references to one
address; each library's fatbin constructor registers that address with
its own code object, and HIP keeps the first registration. rocSPARSE's
constructor runs first, and the stock rocSPARSE has no gfx803 code object
("Cannot find CO in the bundle .../librocsparse.so ... gfx803" at
AMD_LOG_LEVEL=1), so the launch finds no kernel. The HIP log shows the
launch returning hipErrorInvalidDeviceFunction after a few microseconds,
without loading code; dladdr names the handle
(rocprim::detail::init_lookback_scan_state_kernel<lookback_scan_state<
unsigned int, false, true>> for nonzero, rocprim::detail::transform_kernel
<..., float, ..., identity<float>> for sort). The same kernels compiled
into a standalone gfx803 program
(tools/correctness-suite/pytorch/rocprim_repro.hip) work, and rocPRIM kernels that
rocSPARSE does not export (the reduce kernel nonzero launches first) work
inside torch. On GPUs that rocSPARSE has code for, rocSPARSE's identical
copy runs instead and nothing is visibly wrong.

PyTorch avoids the same clash for CUDA's CUB with CUB_WRAPPED_NAMESPACE;
rocPRIM 3.4 has no equivalent option.

WHAT
cmake/Dependencies.cmake adds -Drocprim=at_hip_rocprim to HIP_CXX_FLAGS,
so every rocPRIM name compiled into torch (directly, through hipCUB and
through rocThrust) moves to at_hip_rocprim. Include paths are header-name
tokens and are not macro-expanded, and torch's own sources never name
rocprim, so nothing else changes. Kernel handles then no longer collide
with any other library's. torchvision and torchaudio export no rocPRIM
symbols.

REAL-HARDWARE VERIFICATION (RX 590, this image)
libtorch_hip exports no rocprim symbol (5722 at_hip_rocprim) and shares no
kernel name with librocsparse. tools/correctness-suite/pytorch/
rocprim_ops_check.py (nonzero, masked indexing, unique, sorts and argsorts
in fp32, fp16 and int64 up to 3M elements, cumsum, top-k, index_put,
bincount, searchsorted, randperm, embedding backward) goes from 67 to 118
of 118 cases against the CPU, together with
gfx803-index-put-warp-size.patch. PyTorch op suite 118 of 118, SD 1.5 UNet check stages 1 and 2,
conv checks fp32 and fp16 0 bad, 20 LoRA training steps (median 4 s/step,
loss 0.219), 0 GPU faults.

diff --git a/cmake/Dependencies.cmake b/cmake/Dependencies.cmake
index 7838d32..1c98de7 100644
--- a/cmake/Dependencies.cmake
+++ b/cmake/Dependencies.cmake
@@ -1014,6 +1014,13 @@ if(USE_ROCM)
     list(APPEND HIP_CXX_FLAGS -D__HIP_PLATFORM_AMD__=1)
     list(APPEND HIP_CXX_FLAGS -DCUDA_HAS_FP16=1)
     list(APPEND HIP_CXX_FLAGS -DUSE_ROCM)
+    # rocPRIM is header-only, so every library built with it exports its own copies of rocPRIM's
+    # kernels under the same names; ROCm's rocSPARSE does. The dynamic linker binds them all to one
+    # address, HIP keeps the first registration for it, and when that library has no code object for
+    # the GPU (stock rocSPARSE on gfx803) torch's launches fail with "invalid device function".
+    # A private namespace keeps torch's copies apart, as CUB_WRAPPED_NAMESPACE does for CUDA.
+    # GFX803_ROCPRIM_PRIVATE_NAMESPACE_PATCH
+    list(APPEND HIP_CXX_FLAGS -Drocprim=at_hip_rocprim)
     list(APPEND HIP_CXX_FLAGS -D__HIP_NO_HALF_OPERATORS__=1)
     list(APPEND HIP_CXX_FLAGS -D__HIP_NO_HALF_CONVERSIONS__=1)
     list(APPEND HIP_CXX_FLAGS -DTORCH_HIP_VERSION=${TORCH_HIP_VERSION})
